- **Norvane looks fine.** Caldria Ratings has it as stable, and it has already shown it can issue.\n- **Pellham is the problem.** It is the largest buyer of grid equipment in Caldria. Its C$2.4B equity offering was pulled on 14 October, and Caldria Ratings then put it on negative watch. Debt is the fallback for the funding gap, but a negative watch raises the risk of a downgrade and wider spreads. A downgrade could also limit how much debt it can add.\n- **Pellham is already cutting its plan.** It said it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and resize its Q1 2027 tender. That suggests it can't fully fund the plan as filed, even with debt.\n- **Equity is not an alternative right now.** Two utility share offerings were cut back in Q3, and the equity window looks effectively shut (see n6.r1).\n\n**Gaps in the evidence:**\n- I found no data on debt maturities, leverage or rating headroom.\n- I didn't see the capex amounts in the utility tender calendar.\n- I don't know how much Pellham has already raised or has in committed credit lines.\n\nThose gaps limit how firm the Pellham conclusion is.\n\n**Implication for grid-equipment demand:** a debt-funded 2027 plan holds up for Norvane. Pellham's orders are being reduced. Because Pellham is the biggest single buyer, that matters most for suppliers.","ttft_ms":2730,"type":"result","duration_ms":40017,"uuid":"cc4953af-fa58-4cb2-9e7c-03805ce45e37","ttft_stream_ms":2224,"time_to_request_ms":57,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":4,"system_prompt":4,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":11,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":2,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":5,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":2,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":6,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":1,"tool_schema_build":3,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":2,"request_build":4},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"tim